    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> less for its wealth, since it found no gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> there, than as a fortified buffer between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> and the English colonies to the north.</li><li>Disease, war and the raids of Carolina colonists and their native allies destroyed the native peoples and the Franciscan missions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> refugees and people fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> later formed the Seminole culture in the emptied land.</li><li>From 1693 the Spanish Crown freed enslaved people who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and accepted baptism, and a free black militia garrisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-mose">Fort Mose</a> near St. Augustine.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The marriage of Luisa de Abrego, a free black domestic servant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, and Miguel Rodríguez, a Segovian conquistador, in St. Augustine in 1565 is the first known and recorded Christian marriage in what is now the continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

