ss="rf-year">1586</span><span class="rf-text">The English privateer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> plunders and burns St. Augustine</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1672–1695</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castillo-de-san-marcos">Castillo de San Marcos</a> is built at St. Augustine, with forced labour from the Timucuan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guale">Guale</a> and Apalache peoples</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1702–1704</span><span class="rf-text">James Moore's Carolina army razes the town of St. Augustine and then destroys most of the Apalachee missions</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1763</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> trades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, and the Spanish population of St. Augustine emigrates to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1783</span><span class="rf-text">The Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> returns East and West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, without specifying their boundaries</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1819</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty">Adams–Onís Treaty</a> cedes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>; it takes effect on 17 July 1821</span></li></ol>
