    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, sighted the east coast of the peninsula in April 1513, claimed it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and named it La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, for the season of Pascua Florida. The legend that he sought a Fountain of Youth arose long after his death; like other conquistadors he was most likely looking for gold, land to rule and Indians to convert or enslave. Spanish raiders had probably already enslaved people on the coast. The expeditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panfilo-de-narvaez">Pánfilo de Narváez</a>, who landed near Tampa Bay in 1528, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a>, who marched from Tampa Bay in 1539 as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, found no riches, and attempts to settle, at San Miguel de Gualdape in 1526 and at Ochuse and Santa Elena under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tristan-de-luna-y-arellano">Tristán de Luna y Arellano</a> in 1559, ended in failure.</p><p>Permanent settlement answered French Florida. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-goulaine-de-laudonniere">René Goulaine de Laudonnière</a> founded Fort Caroline for Huguenot colonists in 1564, King Philip II appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a> Adelantado of Florida, with a commission to drive out non-Spanish adventurers. On 8 September 1565 Menéndez landed with settlers to found St. Augustine and the Mission Nombre de Dios. He overwhelmed Fort Caroline, sparing only the women and children, and ordered the shipwrecked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-ribault">Jean Ribault</a>'s fleet executed at the place called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas">Matanzas</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>With no gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, and a native population too reduced by disease and slave raids for an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> system of forced farm labour, Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> had no large plantations. Free-range <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranches in north-central Florida were its most successful enterprise, supplying local and Cuban markets; Francisco Menéndez Márquez founded the largest. From the 1630s a chain of missions from St. Augustine to the panhandle sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and other food to the town, and Apalachee men from the missions had to work there every year. The ports of St. Augustine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> served Spanish ships in need of water and supplies.</p><p>Spanish men married or had unions with Pensacola, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> and African women, slave and free, and their children formed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizo</a> and mulatto population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> encouraged people enslaved in the southern colonies to come to Florida as a refuge: St. Augustine had an all-black militia by 1683, and in 1693 King Charles II proclaimed free all who fled to Florida and accepted conversion and baptism. A free black population grew in St. Augustine, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-mose">Fort Mose</a> was settled by free black militiamen and their families. Spain ended the policy in 1790, but people fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> kept crossing the border to settle near Seminole villages.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The colony's defence rested on a few fortified towns. In 1586 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> plundered and burned St. Augustine on his way back from raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> and Cartagena, exposing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s inability to defend its settlements. From 1672 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castillo-de-san-marcos">Castillo de San Marcos</a> was built there with the forced labour of Timucuan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guale">Guale</a> and Apalache people, its first stage completed in 1695, with Fort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas">Matanzas</a> to the south to watch the sea. In 1702 James Moore's army of Carolina colonists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamasee">Yamasee</a>, Tallapoosa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> warriors razed the town but could not take the fort; in 1704 his raids into Apalachee Province destroyed most of the remaining missions and killed or enslaved most of their people.</p><p>On the Gulf coast Spain tried to block French expansion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>. It founded the Presidio Santa Maria de Galve on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> Bay in 1696 and a presidio on St. Joseph Bay in 1701; the French took both in 1718 and 1719, and Spain regained Pensacola Bay in 1722. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-jenkins-ear">War of Jenkins' Ear</a> (1739–1748) a British attack on St. Augustine and a Spanish invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> were both repulsed. By the early 19th century there were only about 500 Spanish troops in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, and in 1814 the Spanish governor at Pensacola let British forces land to arm the Creeks against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> formed part of the Captaincy General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">Viceroyalty of New Spain</a>. Its governor sat at St. Augustine, but the Gulf presidios founded from 1696 were placed directly under the Viceroy of New Spain, while the Presidio San Marcos de Apalachee, founded in 1718, answered to the governor. Several governors and officials were born in the province, among them Nicolás Ponce de León II, acting governor in the 1660s and 1670s, and Pedro Benedit Horruytiner, governor from 1651 to 1654.</p><p>Spanish control rested on alliances with native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">caciques</a>, who allied with the Spanish to strengthen their power, gain protection against rivals and acquire goods; these alliances were unstable. Governor Pedro de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibarra">Ibarra</a> sought peace with the peoples south of St. Augustine and in 1605 sent the cartographer Álvaro Mexía to the Ais. The missions were the other instrument of rule: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> began them in 1567 and withdrew in 1572, and from 1573 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> ran dozens among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guale">Guale</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timucua">Timucua</a> and Apalachee. The Guale rebelled in 1597, the Apalachee in February 1647, after which their men were forced to work on public projects and Spanish ranches, and the Timucua in 1656. In the second Spanish period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> kept the British division into East and West Florida, and in 1785 moved their border east to the Suwannee River.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> was the first major European land claim and attempted settlement in northern America. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s claim, resting on the 16th-century expeditions, once reached over much of the present south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and Juan Pardo's expeditions from Santa Elena in 1566–1568 went as far as eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>. St. Augustine is the oldest continuously inhabited European-established settlement in the continental United States, and Mission Nombre de Dios was perhaps the first mission there.</p><p>For the native peoples Spanish rule meant war, forced labour and, above all, epidemic disease. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timucua">Timucua</a>, Calusa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tequesta">Tequesta</a>, Apalachee, Tocobaga and Ais, most of whom had resisted the Spanish, declined drastically, and by the early 1700s large parts of the peninsula were nearly empty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> and other refugees driven from their lands by the Carolina settlements moved south into this land in the 18th century, joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>; with perhaps a few descendants of the older peoples they formed a new Seminole culture. Under pressure from settlers and the Seminole Wars the Seminole later moved into central and southern Florida. The colony's role as a refuge from slavery made it a source of conflict with the southern colonies and then with the United States.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> shrank from the 1600s as native depopulation emptied the missions. From 1680 Carolina colonists and their allies attacked mission villages, and after Moore's raids of 1702–1704 the missionaries abandoned their outposts in 1706. The Province of Carolina, French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> from 1718 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from 1732 hemmed in its borders. In 1763 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> traded Florida to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, and the entire Spanish population of St. Augustine left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. Spain recaptured most of West Florida in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, and the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> of 1783 returned both Floridas to it.</p><p>The ill-defined borders brought a dispute with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, settled at the 31st parallel by Pinckney's Treaty of 1795. The United States intervened in a West Florida uprising in 1810, absorbed the Mobile District by 1812, and quietly supported the Patriot War of East Florida of 1810–1812. American slave-hunters raided Black Seminole villages; the 'Negro Fort' at Prospect Bluff was destroyed by an American attack in 1816, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a>'s campaign of 1817–1818, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-seminole-war">First Seminole War</a>, occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a>. Unable to defend the territory, Spain negotiated: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty">Adams–Onís Treaty</a> of 22 February 1819 took effect on 17 July 1821, and hundreds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-seminoles">Black Seminoles</a> sailed from Cape Florida to the Bahamas to avoid being returned to bondage.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> was founded at St. Augustine in 1565 to keep the French and English from the routes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. Without gold or a large labour force it remained a buffer of forts, missions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranches; its native peoples were destroyed by disease and slave raids, and after a British interlude it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1821.</em></p>

